    {
      "id": "cb9-l2",
      "title": "Conditions and 88 Levels",
      "xp": 15,
      "intro": [
        "Conditions combine with AND, OR and NOT. COBOL even lets you leave out a repeated subject: IF CODE = \"A\" OR \"B\" means CODE = \"A\" OR CODE = \"B\".",
        "An 88 level gives a name to values of a field: under 01 STATUS-CODE, 88 ACTIVE VALUE \"A\" makes IF ACTIVE mean IF STATUS-CODE = \"A\". It reads like English, and SET ACTIVE TO TRUE puts the \"A\" in.",
        "Class tests check what a field holds: IF AMOUNT IS NUMERIC, IF BALANCE IS NEGATIVE."
      ],
      "example": {
        "code": [
          "       IDENTIFICATION DIVISION.",
          "       PROGRAM-ID. STATUS.",
          "       DATA DIVISION.",
          "       WORKING-STORAGE SECTION.",
          "       01 ACCT-STATUS  PIC X VALUE \"A\".",
          "          88 ACTIVE   VALUE \"A\".",
          "          88 CLOSED   VALUE \"C\".",
          "          88 FROZEN   VALUE \"F\".",
          "       01 GRADE        PIC 9 VALUE 2.",
          "       PROCEDURE DIVISION.",
          "           IF ACTIVE",
          "               DISPLAY \"ACCOUNT IS ACTIVE\"",
          "           END-IF.",
          "           SET CLOSED TO TRUE.",
          "           IF CLOSED OR FROZEN",
          "               DISPLAY \"NOW: \" ACCT-STATUS",
          "           END-IF.",
          "           IF GRADE >= 1 AND GRADE <= 3",
          "               DISPLAY \"GOOD CUSTOMER\"",
          "           END-IF.",
          "           STOP RUN."
        ],
        "output": [
          "ACCOUNT IS ACTIVE",
          "NOW: C",
          "GOOD CUSTOMER"
        ]
      },
      "exercises": [
        {
          "id": "cb9-l2-e1",
